{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggV3MNeqw565LMMy1pjk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For this complex, **Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**. C cannot be found from these bands.\n\n**How the bands are assigned**\n\nA tetrahedral d⁷ ion behaves like an octahedral d³ ion. The ground term is ⁴A₂(F), and there are three spin-allowed transitions:\n\n- ν₁: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F). Its energy equals 10Dq.\n- ν₂: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F).\n- ν₃: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P).\n\nThe two ⁴T₁ levels mix through configuration interaction. Measured from the ground state, their energies are:\n\nE(⁴T₁) = 7.5B + 15Dq ∓ ½·√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n\n**Solving for the parameters**\n\n1. From ν₁: 10Dq = 4070 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 407 cm⁻¹**.\n2. Adding the two ⁴T₁ energies cancels the square root: ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B.\n   - 7030 + 16400 = 23430 = 12210 + 15B\n   - So **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Check**\n\nThe difference between the two ⁴T₁ bands should equal the square-root term:\n\n- Observed: ν₃ − ν₂ = 9370 cm⁻¹.\n- Calculated: √(225·748² − 180·748·407 + 100·407²) ≈ 9362 cm⁻¹.\n\nThese agree to within about 8 cm⁻¹, so the assignment holds together.\n\nFor comparison, the free-ion value of B for Co²⁺ is about 1120 cm⁻¹. This gives β ≈ 0.67, which is a typical amount of covalent reduction (nephelauxetic effect) for a tetrahedral complex.\n\n**Why C is left out**\n\nAll three bands are quartet-to-quartet transitions, and their energies depend only on Dq and B. C would need spin-forbidden (doublet) bands, so it is reported as null.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Quartet bands give Dq from nu1 and B from nu2+nu3; C needs spin-forbidden bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1648,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1648,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens